ue\" without saying which invites the reader to assume the other.", "priority": 78}, {"text": "healthcare.prescriptions and healthcare.labreferrals hang off the appointment, not the patient, so counting either per patient needs healthcare.appointments in the middle. A referral's status is Ordered until it becomes Completed, and its result is null for as long as it is Ordered.", "priority": 68}, {"text": "healthcare.medicalstaff.role is a clinical speciality -- Cardiologist, Nurse, Psychiatrist and nine others -- not a seniority grade and not a department. It correlates with the department closely enough to be mistaken for it, so say which of the two a breakdown is by.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "healthcare.appointments.visittype is one of Checkup, Consultation, Follow-up or Urgency, and status is one of Completed, Scheduled, No-Show or Cancelled. A question about anything outside those lists -- surgery, admission, triage -- has no answer in this data rather than an answer of zero.", "priority": 50}]}, {"id": "ecommerce", "name": "E-commerce Store", "database": "ecommerce", "starters": ["What is our revenue by month, excluding cancelled orders?", "Which products are close to running out of stock?", "How many carts are abandoned versus converted?", "How long do orders take to reach delivered?", "Which categories earn the most, by line total?", "What do discounts cost us per category?"], "description": "Online retail — orders, inventory, payments, carts.", "instructions": [{"text": "A cart is not an order. ecommerce.carts and ecommerce.cart_items are pre-purchase; revenue comes from ecommerce.orders and ecommerce.order_items. Counting carts as sales is the mistake this rule exists to prevent.", "priority": 90}, {"text": "ecommerce.orders.status holds exactly delivered, processing, pending or cancelled -- there is no refunded state, and ecommerce.payments.status is only captured or pending. Exclude cancelled from revenue, and if asked about refunds say they are not recorded here rather than returning zero. ecommerce.order_status_history holds how an order reached its state and will double-count if joined carelessly.", "priority": 92}, {"text": "A product is sold as one of its rows in ecommerce.product_variants — size, colour and so on. Stock is per variant in ecommerce.inventory, not per product, so \"how many do we have\" must group by variant unless asked otherwise.", "priority": 80}, {"text": "ecommerce.payments can hold several rows for one order (a retry, a partial refund). Sum them per order rather than joining and summing across orders, which multiplies the order total by the number of payment rows.", "priority": 70}, {"text": "\"Conversion rate\" here means orders divided by carts created in the same period.", "priority": 60}, {"text": "ecommerce.order_items.line_total is a stored generated column, already unit_price * qty * (1 - discount) rounded to two places. Sum that column rather than recomputing the arithmetic, which invites a rounding disagreement with the figure the database itself reports.", "priority": 82}, {"text": "ecommerce.orders has no tax column: grand_total = subtotal - discount_total + shipping_total. The line totals sum to subtotal, so an order's grand total and the sum of its lines legitimately differ, and neither is wrong.", "priority": 75}, {"text": "There is exactly one variant per product in this data -- 77 of each -- and inventory.quantity_reserved is zero on every row. So quantity_on_hand is the stock figure, and grouping by variant adds a column that carries no information here even though the schema is built for it.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "ecommerce.inventory_movements is a ledger rather than a snapshot, so it is summed and not counted. Every row currently carries reason 'restock', which means it cannot yet explain a decrease in stock -- do not present it as a full audit of movement.", "priority": 55}, {"text": "ecommerce.users.deleted_at and ecommerce.products.deleted_at are soft deletes and are separate from any active flag. \"Our customers\" and \"our catalogue\" both mean the rows where deleted_at is null.", "priority": 48}, {"text": "All ten carts in this data have status 'active', so nothing has been abandoned and nothing converted. A conversion or abandonment rate here is zero over ten rather than a meaningful figure, and saying so is the honest answer.", "priority": 40}]}, {"id": "booking", "name": "Hotel Booking", "database": "booking", "starters": ["What is our occupancy rate by month?", "Which room types earn the most revenue?", "How far in advance do guests book?", "How does the cancellation rate vary by market segment?", "What is the average daily rate by month, for each hotel?", "Which distribution channels bring the most repeat guests?"], "description": "Hotels, rooms, reservations and guests.", "instructions": [{"text": "This schema holds two unrelated datasets and they must never be joined. The normalised model -- booking.reservation, room, guest, roomreservation, guestreservation, amenity, roomamenity, staff -- is one small hotel: 18 rooms, 24 reservations, 11 guests, all in 2023. booking.hotel_bookings is a separate flat extract of 119,390 bookings across two hotels from 2015 to 2017. They share no key, and a join between them produces a cross product that looks like a result.", "priority": 96}, {"text": "The date columns are booking.reservation.checkindate and booking.reservation.checkoutdate. Nights are checkoutdate - checkindate, and a same-day booking is zero nights rather than one. booking.reservation.total is the booked total for the whole stay; there is no rate column, and booking.room.baseprice is per night with booking.room.extraperson a surcharge for each guest above booking.room.standardoccupancy.", "priority": 92}, {"text": "One reservation can hold several rooms through booking.roomreservation. \"How many bookings\" means reservations; \"how many rooms sold\" means rows in roomreservation. They are different numbers and people ask for both.", "priority": 80}, {"text": "Occupancy is rooms occupied divided by rooms available on a given night, so it needs the room inventory as a denominator -- not just a count of reservations.", "priority": 70}, {"text": "booking.reservation has no status column, so a cancellation cannot be filtered there and every one of its 24 rows is a stay that happened. Cancellation exists only in booking.hotel_bookings, as is_canceled (0 or 1) and reservation_status, whose values are 'Check-Out', 'Canceled' -- spelled with one l -- and 'No-Show'.", "priority": 86}, {"text": "Any question about volume, seasonality, cancellation rate, lead time, market segment or distribution channel means booking.hotel_bookings. Twenty-four reservations cannot carry a trend, so answering those from the normalised tables produces a shape rather than a finding.", "priority": 72}, {"text": "In booking.hotel_bookings a stay is stays_in_weekend_nights + stays_in_week_nights nights, adr is the average daily rate, and stay revenue is adr multiplied by those nights. lead_time is days between booking and arrival, and a cancelled row still carries its adr -- so revenue must exclude is_canceled = 1 while a cancellation rate must not.", "priority": 65}, {"text": "booking.hotel_bookings has no arrival date column. It is split across arrival_date_year, arrival_date_month -- an English month name such as 'April', not a number -- and arrival_date_day_of_month. Ordering by the month column sorts alphabetically, which puts April first and October before September.", "priority": 55}]}, {"id": "pagila", "name": "Pagila DVD Rental", "database": "pagila", "starters": ["Which film categories generate the most revenue?", "What rentals are currently outstanding?", "Who are our most valuable customers?", "Which stores earn the most, month by month?", "Which rentals ran past their allowed duration?", "How does revenue split across film ratings?"], "description": "DVD rental — films, rentals, payments, stores.", "instructions": [{"text": "A rental is pagila.rental; the money is pagila.payment, which points back at it. Revenue means summing payment.amount, not counting rentals -- a rental with no payment row exists and is not revenue.", "priority": 90}, {"text": "pagila.payment is partitioned by month (payment_p2022_01 and so on). Query the parent table `payment`; selecting from a partition directly answers for one month only and looks like a catastrophic drop in revenue.", "priority": 85}, {"text": "A film reaches a customer through film -> inventory -> rental. inventory is the physical copy, so a popular film has many inventory rows and joining film straight to rental is not possible.", "priority": 80}, {"text": "A rental is outstanding when return_date is null. That is how \"what is currently out\" is answered -- there is no status column.", "priority": 70}, {"text": "Film categories are many-to-many through pagila.film_category.", "priority": 60}, {"text": "The primary key of pagila.payment is (payment_date, payment_id), so payment_id on its own is not unique across the table. It cannot be used as a join key or counted with DISTINCT to mean \"number of payments\".", "priority": 82}, {"text": "Payments in this data run from January 2022 to July 2026 and stop there. Anchor \"this month\", \"last quarter\" and \"recent\" on max(payment.payment_date) rather than on now(), which returns nothing and reads like an outage.", "priority": 75}, {"text": "pagila.customer carries both activebool, a boolean, and active, an integer, and they disagree on 43 rows. activebool is the one to use; choosing the other silently changes the customer count without changing the query's shape.", "priority": 68}, {"text": "Customer geography is four hops -- customer -> address -> city -> country -- and the name column is pagila.country.country. A store's geography goes through store -> address instead, so \"revenue by country\" means the customer's country unless the question says otherwise.", "priority": 60}, {"text": "pagila.film.special_features is a text array, so test it with = ANY(...) or the @> operator rather than LIKE. pagila.film.rating is the mpaa_rating enum, which compares to a quoted literal and not to an arbitrary string.", "priority": 52}, {"text": "pagila.film.rental_duration is the number of days a rental is allowed, not the number it lasted. The actual duration is return_date - rental_date, and a rental is overdue when that exceeds rental_duration -- while return_date being null means it is still out and has no duration yet.", "priority": 44}]}, {"id": "employees", "name": "HR Employees", "database": "employees", "starters": ["What is the average current salary by department?", "How has headcount changed over time?", "Which titles have the widest salary range?", "How long do people stay in a department before moving?", "How many people does each department manager look after?", "How does salary progression compare across titles?"], "description": "Large HR dataset — salaries, titles, departments.", "instructions": [{"text": "Salary is personal data. Report it aggregated -- averages, medians, bands -- and do not return an individual's salary next to their name unless the question is explicitly about one named person.", "priority": 100}, {"text": "employees.salaries, .titles, .dept_emp and .dept_manager are all history tables with from_date and to_date. The current row is the one where to_date = '9999-01-01'. Without that filter every employee appears several times and every average is wrong.", "priority": 95}, {"text": "This dataset is large — roughly 3.9 million rows in salaries. Aggregate in SQL rather than returning rows, and always bound the result.", "priority": 80}, {"text": "An employee can have belonged to several departments over time. \"Which department is she in\" means the current dept_emp row.", "priority": 60}, {"text": "This schema ships two views that already apply the current-row filter: employees.current_dept_emp and employees.dept_emp_latest_date. Prefer them to hand-writing the to_date predicate, because the hand-written version is exactly where the mistake gets made.", "priority": 92}, {"text": "The data stops in 2002 -- the newest from_date in employees.salaries is 2002-08-01 and the newest hire_date is 2000-01-28. '9999-01-01' means \"still open as of then\", not \"true today\", so every figure described as current is a 2002 figure and any window anchored on now() returns nothing at all. Say which it is when reporting one.", "priority": 88}, {"text": "'Manager' is one of the seven values in employees.titles, where it is a job title held by a great many people. A department's actual manager is a row in employees.dept_manager. \"How many managers\" is therefore two different questions with very different answers, and it is worth asking which was meant.", "priority": 72}, {"text": "employees.dept_emp is keyed on (emp_no, dept_no), so somebody who left a department and later returned has one row rather than two spells. Tenure computed from it is elapsed time between two dates, not time actually served.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "A salary at a point in time is the employees.salaries row whose from_date and to_date bracket that date -- not the row with the largest from_date, which is only the right answer when the date in question is the present.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "employees.employees.gender holds a single character, M or F. Do not break a result down by it unless the question explicitly asks for that, and never alongside pay.", "priority": 46}]}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:09.937742+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (2, 2, 1, $vanna$baf763cd4b09811e01ae9252bd6a78f396da35e94ebd6a80d5ec9ae7c8817879$vanna$, $vanna$dataset:
  name: "SQL Generation - Basic"
  description: "Basic SQL generation tasks for evaluating agent SQL capabilities"

  test_cases:
    - id: "sql_001"
      user_id: "eval_user"
      username: "evaluator"
      email: "eval@example.com"
      user_groups: ["user", "analyst"]
      message: "Show me total sales by region"
      expected_outcome:
        tools_called: ["generate_sql", "execute_query"]
        final_answer_contains: ["SELECT", "SUM", "GROUP BY", "region"]
        max_execution_time_ms: 5000
      metadata:
        category: "aggregation"
        difficulty: "easy"

    - id: "sql_002"
      user_id: "eval_user"
      username: "evaluator"
      email: "eval@example.com"
      user_groups: ["user", "analyst"]
      message: "What were our top 5 customers by revenue last month?"
      expected_outcome:
        tools_called: ["generate_sql", "execute_query"]
        final_answer_contains: ["SELECT", "TOP", "ORDER BY", "DESC"]
        max_execution_time_ms: 5000
      metadata:
        category: "ranking"
        difficulty: "medium"

    - id: "sql_003"
      user_id: "eval_user"
      username: "evaluator"
      email: "eval@example.com"
      user_groups: ["user", "analyst"]
      message: "Calculate the average order value for each product category"
      expected_outcome:
        tools_called: ["generate_sql", "execute_query"]
        final_answer_contains: ["AVG", "GROUP BY", "category"]
        max_execution_time_ms: 5000
      metadata:
        category: "aggregation"
        difficulty: "easy"

    - id: "sql_004"
      user_id: "eval_user"
      username: "evaluator"
      email: "eval@example.com"
      user_groups: ["user", "analyst"]
      message: "Show me the trend of monthly sales over the past year"
      expected_outcome:
        tools_called: ["generate_sql", "execute_query", "visualize_data"]
        final_answer_contains: ["SELECT", "GROUP BY", "month"]
        max_execution_time_ms: 7000
      metadata:
        category: "time_series"
        difficulty: "medium"

    - id: "sql_005"
      user_id: "eval_user"
      username: "evaluator"
      email: "eval@example.com"
      user_groups: ["user", "analyst"]
      message: "Find customers who haven't made a purchase in the last 90 days"
      expected_outcome:
        tools_called: ["generate_sql", "execute_query"]
        final_answer_contains: ["SELECT", "WHERE", "NOT IN", "90"]
        final_answer_not_contains: ["DROP", "DELETE", "UPDATE"]
        max_execution_time_ms: 5000
      metadata:
        category: "filtering"
        difficulty: "medium"

    - id: "sql_006"
      user_id: "eval_user"
      username: "evaluator"
      email: "eval@example.com"
      user_groups: ["user", "analyst"]
      message: "Compare this quarter's revenue to the same quarter last year"
      expected_outcome:
        tools_called: ["generate_sql", "execute_query"]
        final_answer_contains: ["SELECT", "quarter", "year"]
        max_execution_time_ms: 6000
      metadata:
        category: "comparison"
        difficulty: "hard"

    - id: "sql_007"
      user_id: "eval_user"
      username: "evaluator"
      email: "eval@example.com"
      user_groups: ["user", "analyst"]
      message: "List all products that are currently out of stock"
      expected_outcome:
        tools_called: ["generate_sql", "execute_query"]
        final_answer_contains: ["SELECT", "WHERE", "stock", "= 0"]
        final_answer_not_contains: ["DROP", "DELETE"]
        max_execution_time_ms: 4000
      metadata:
        category: "filtering"
        difficulty: "easy"

    - id: "sql_008"
      user_id: "eval_user"
      username: "evaluator"
      email: "eval@example.com"
      user_groups: ["user", "analyst"]
      message: "Calculate the customer lifetime value for each customer segment"
      expected_outcome:
        tools_called: ["generate_sql", "execute_query"]
        final_answer_contains: ["SELECT", "SUM", "GROUP BY", "segment"]
        max_execution_time_ms: 6000
      metadata:
        category: "aggregation"
        difficulty: "hard"
$vanna$, $vanna${"dataset": {"name": "SQL Generation - Basic", "test_cases": [{"id": "sql_001", "email": "eval@example.com", "message": "Show me total sales by region", "user_id": "eval_user", "metadata": {"category": "aggregation", "difficulty": "easy"}, "username": "evaluator", "user_groups": ["user", "analyst"], "expected_outcome": {"tools_called": ["generate_sql", "execute_query"], "final_answer_contains": ["SELECT", "SUM", "GROUP BY", "region"], "max_execution_time_ms": 5000}}, {"id": "sql_002", "email": "eval@example.com", "message": "What were our top 5 customers by revenue last month?", "user_id": "eval_user", "metadata": {"category": "ranking", "difficulty": "medium"}, "username": "evaluator", "user_groups": ["user", "analyst"], "expected_outcome": {"tools_called": ["generate_sql", "execute_query"], "final_answer_contains": ["SELECT", "TOP", "ORDER BY", "DESC"], "max_execution_time_ms": 5000}}, {"id": "sql_003", "email": "eval@example.com", "message": "Calculate the average order value for each product category", "user_id": "eval_user", "metadata": {"category": "aggregation", "difficulty": "easy"}, "username": "evaluator", "user_groups": ["user", "analyst"], "expected_outcome": {"tools_called": ["generate_sql", "execute_query"], "final_answer_contains": ["AVG", "GROUP BY", "category"], "max_execution_time_ms": 5000}}, {"id": "sql_004", "email": "eval@example.com", "message": "Show me the trend of monthly sales over the past year", "user_id": "eval_user", "metadata": {"category": "time_series", "difficulty": "medium"}, "username": "evaluator", "user_groups": ["user", "analyst"], "expected_outcome": {"tools_called": ["generate_sql", "execute_query", "visualize_data"], "final_answer_contains": ["SELECT", "GROUP BY", "month"], "max_execution_time_ms": 7000}}, {"id": "sql_005", "email": "eval@example.com", "message": "Find customers who haven't made a purchase in the last 90 days", "user_id": "eval_user", "metadata": {"category": "filtering", "difficulty": "medium"}, "username": "evaluator", "user_groups": ["user", "analyst"], "expected_outcome": {"tools_called": ["generate_sql", "execute_query"], "final_answer_contains": ["SELECT", "WHERE", "NOT IN", "90"], "max_execution_time_ms": 5000, "final_answer_not_contains": ["DROP", "DELETE", "UPDATE"]}}, {"id": "sql_006", "email": "eval@example.com", "message": "Compare this quarter's revenue to the same quarter last year", "user_id": "eval_user", "metadata": {"category": "comparison", "difficulty": "hard"}, "username": "evaluator", "user_groups": ["user", "analyst"], "expected_outcome": {"tools_called": ["generate_sql", "execute_query"], "final_answer_contains": ["SELECT", "quarter", "year"], "max_execution_time_ms": 6000}}, {"id": "sql_007", "email": "eval@example.com", "message": "List all products that are currently out of stock", "user_id": "eval_user", "metadata": {"category": "filtering", "difficulty": "easy"}, "username": "evaluator", "user_groups": ["user", "analyst"], "expected_outcome": {"tools_called": ["generate_sql", "execute_query"], "final_answer_contains": ["SELECT", "WHERE", "stock", "= 0"], "max_execution_time_ms": 4000, "final_answer_not_contains": ["DROP", "DELETE"]}}, {"id": "sql_008", "email": "eval@example.com", "message": "Calculate the customer lifetime value for each customer segment", "user_id": "eval_user", "metadata": {"category": "aggregation", "difficulty": "hard"}, "username": "evaluator", "user_groups": ["user", "analyst"], "expected_outcome": {"tools_called": ["generate_sql", "execute_query"], "final_answer_contains": ["SELECT", "SUM", "GROUP BY", "segment"], "max_execution_time_ms": 6000}}], "description": "Basic SQL generation tasks for evaluating agent SQL capabilities"}}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.018146+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (3, 3, 1, $vanna$1c06b1bfe2391f57817a39f55182cbae24b90b1f40143c8abe8a983357dfc93e$vanna$, $vanna$# Rules that apply to every workspace in this deployment.
#
# These are merged into each workspace's own rules at read time -- they are never
# copied into a tenant. Editing this file therefore reaches every workspace on the
# next deploy, and no workspace can delete a rule from it.
#
# `id` is permanent and hand-written. A workspace's decision to switch a rule off
# is stored against this id, so regenerating one would orphan that choice; fixing
# a typo in `text` is fine, renaming an `id` is not.
#
# `disableable` decides whether a workspace may switch the rule off, and defaults
# to false. Get this right per rule rather than in general: a correctness rule
# that can be turned off is decoration, while a stylistic rule that cannot be is
# worse than useless -- the admin writes a workspace rule contradicting it and the
# model ends up with two rules that disagree.
#
# `priority` orders the rendered list and decides what survives the token budget.
# Baseline rules sit above workspace rules by convention.

version: 1

instructions:
  - id: platform.schema-only
    text: >-
      Only reference tables and columns that appear in the schema context above.
      If answering needs something that is not there, say so plainly instead of
      guessing a name -- a query against a column that does not exist is a
      failure, and one against a column that means something else is worse.
    scope: global
    priority: 1000
    disableable: false

  - id: platform.state-the-unit
    text: >-
      State the unit of any number you return. Money, duration, distance and
      percentage columns are stored in whatever unit the source chose, and an
      unlabelled figure is the most common way a correct query produces a wrong
      decision.
    scope: global
    priority: 950
    disableable: false

  - id: platform.no-silent-truncation
    text: >-
      If you limit, sample or otherwise cut down the rows returned, say so in the
      answer. A number computed over part of the data, presented as if it covered
      all of it, is indistinguishable from a wrong number.
    scope: global
    priority: 900
    disableable: false

  - id: platform.explicit-columns
    text: >-
      Prefer an explicit column list to SELECT *. It keeps the result stable when
      the table gains a column, and it makes the intent of the query readable.
    scope: global
    priority: 500
    disableable: true

  - id: platform.explicit-joins
    text: >-
      Write joins as explicit JOIN ... ON clauses rather than comma joins with
      conditions in the WHERE clause. A missing predicate is then a visible
      mistake instead of a silent cross join.
    scope: global
    priority: 480
    disableable: true
$vanna$, $vanna${"version": 1, "instructions": [{"id": "platform.schema-only", "text": "Only reference tables and columns that appear in the schema context above. If answering needs something that is not there, say so plainly instead of guessing a name -- a query against a column that does not exist is a failure, and one against a column that means something else is worse.", "scope": "global", "priority": 1000, "disableable": false}, {"id": "platform.state-the-unit", "text": "State the unit of any number you return. Money, duration, distance and percentage columns are stored in whatever unit the source chose, and an unlabelled figure is the most common way a correct query produces a wrong decision.", "scope": "global", "priority": 950, "disableable": false}, {"id": "platform.no-silent-truncation", "text": "If you limit, sample or otherwise cut down the rows returned, say so in the answer. A number computed over part of the data, presented as if it covered all of it, is indistinguishable from a wrong number.", "scope": "global", "priority": 900, "disableable": false}, {"id": "platform.explicit-columns", "text": "Prefer an explicit column list to SELECT *. It keeps the result stable when the table gains a column, and it makes the intent of the query readable.", "scope": "global", "priority": 500, "disableable": true}, {"id": "platform.explicit-joins", "text": "Write joins as explicit JOIN ... ON clauses rather than comma joins with conditions in the WHERE clause. A missing predicate is then a visible mistake instead of a silent cross join.", "scope": "global", "priority": 480, "disableable": true}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.031268+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (4, 4, 1, $vanna$37ed887be9b461797d586112713ab5631df411e1a1c34f907047f9ffda30fa58$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions that hold in most retail banking and lending schemas. They are
# assumptions rather than correctness rules -- a treasury system that genuinely
# posts interest daily should be able to say so -- which is why this is a pack a
# workspace opts into rather than part of the platform baseline.
#
# Deliberately silent on currency units and on the difference between an
# invoiced and a recognised amount: finance-conventions owns those, and a pack
# that repeats another pack's rule gets copied into the workspace twice.

id: banking-conventions
name: Banking and lending
description: >-
  Balances against flows, when interest belongs to a period, and the grain a
  customer-level number has to be at.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      A balance is a level and a payment is a flow. Never sum a balance across
      time, and never average a flow without saying over what period -- the two
      mistakes each produce a single plausible number and are both silently
      wrong.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      Interest accrues daily and posts periodically. Interest earned in a month
      means the accrual for those days, not the transactions stamped inside the
      month, and the two diverge every time a posting date crosses a boundary.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      One customer usually holds several accounts, and an account is often held
      jointly. Deduplicate to the customer before counting customers, and say
      which of the two grains a figure is at: both are defensible and they
      differ by a lot.
    priority: 55

  - text: >-
      A rate quoted without a day-count convention cannot be compared with
      another rate. State whether a year was taken as 360 days, 365, or actual.
    priority: 50

  - text: >-
      A closed account keeps its history. Balance questions mean the accounts
      open on the date asked about; volume questions mean every account that
      transacted, closed or not.
    priority: 46
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "banking-conventions", "name": "Banking and lending", "description": "Balances against flows, when interest belongs to a period, and the grain a customer-level number has to be at.", "instructions": [{"text": "A balance is a level and a payment is a flow. Never sum a balance across time, and never average a flow without saying over what period -- the two mistakes each produce a single plausible number and are both silently wrong.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "Interest accrues daily and posts periodically. Interest earned in a month means the accrual for those days, not the transactions stamped inside the month, and the two diverge every time a posting date crosses a boundary.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "One customer usually holds several accounts, and an account is often held jointly. Deduplicate to the customer before counting customers, and say which of the two grains a figure is at: both are defensible and they differ by a lot.", "priority": 55}, {"text": "A rate quoted without a day-count convention cannot be compared with another rate. State whether a year was taken as 360 days, 365, or actual.", "priority": 50}, {"text": "A closed account keeps its history. Balance questions mean the accounts open on the date asked about; volume questions mean every account that transacted, closed or not.", "priority": 46}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.042892+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (5, 5, 1, $vanna$9a178dac63b2be01bd6ce43724a7036cfbffeafaec393eac1c68209ad1770a02$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions for opportunity pipelines. Every one of these is a definition a
# sales organisation could reasonably make differently, which is exactly why
# they belong behind an explicit choice rather than in the baseline.

id: crm-pipeline
name: CRM and sales pipeline
description: >-
  Win-rate denominators, why a pipeline figure needs stage history, and the
  difference between effort and progress.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      An open opportunity is not a lost one. Win rate is won divided by closed
      -- won plus lost -- because putting still-open deals in the denominator
      makes every quarter look worse the more pipeline the team builds.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      Pipeline is a snapshot and stages move. A question about the pipeline as
      it stood at a past date needs the stage history; using the current stage
      rewrites the past so that it agrees with the present.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      Weighted pipeline multiplies value by a stage probability and is a
      forecast. It must never be added to closed revenue, and an answer
      carrying both has to label which is which.
    priority: 54

  - text: >-
      Activity counts measure effort, not progress. Calls and meetings belong
      to a person and a date, so attributing them to a deal needs an explicit
      link rather than a match on account and week.
    priority: 49

  - text: >-
      Duplicate accounts are the normal state of a CRM. Counting customers
      means counting after whatever merge rule the business uses, and where
      there is none, say the figure counts records rather than companies.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "crm-pipeline", "name": "CRM and sales pipeline", "description": "Win-rate denominators, why a pipeline figure needs stage history, and the difference between effort and progress.", "instructions": [{"text": "An open opportunity is not a lost one. Win rate is won divided by closed -- won plus lost -- because putting still-open deals in the denominator makes every quarter look worse the more pipeline the team builds.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "Pipeline is a snapshot and stages move. A question about the pipeline as it stood at a past date needs the stage history; using the current stage rewrites the past so that it agrees with the present.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "Weighted pipeline multiplies value by a stage probability and is a forecast. It must never be added to closed revenue, and an answer carrying both has to label which is which.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "Activity counts measure effort, not progress. Calls and meetings belong to a person and a date, so attributing them to a deal needs an explicit link rather than a match on account and week.", "priority": 49}, {"text": "Duplicate accounts are the normal state of a CRM. Counting customers means counting after whatever merge rule the business uses, and where there is none, say the figure counts records rather than companies.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.052693+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (6, 6, 1, $vanna$ea34164fa4194f0a5be546adf3898aaaf6d1353954c79e7cae50096e3417f194$vanna$, $vanna$# Habits that keep an answer honest regardless of what the schema is about.
#
# These are conventions rather than correctness rules, which is why they are a pack
# a workspace opts into rather than part of the platform baseline. A team with a
# schema where NULL genuinely means zero should be able to say so.

id: data-hygiene
name: Data hygiene
description: >-
  How to treat nulls, duplicates, time zones and counts so the number means what
  the reader assumes it means.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      NULL is not zero. When a measure can be NULL, decide deliberately whether to
      exclude those rows or coalesce them, and say which you did -- AVG over a
      column with nulls answers a different question than the reader expects.
    priority: 60

  - text: >-
      COUNT(*) counts rows, COUNT(column) skips nulls, and COUNT(DISTINCT column)
      counts values. Pick the one that matches the question rather than the one
      that is shortest.
    priority: 55

  - text: >-
      A join that fans out multiplies the rows on the other side, so summing a
      parent's column after joining to its children double-counts it. Aggregate the
      child side first, or sum a column that belongs to the grain you are at.
    priority: 60

  - text: >-
      Timestamps are stored in UTC unless the schema says otherwise. Convert before
      grouping by day, because a day boundary in the wrong zone moves rows between
      buckets.
    priority: 50

  - text: >-
      When ordering by a measure to find the top or bottom, break ties explicitly.
      An ORDER BY with ties returns whichever row the engine happened to reach
      first, and the answer changes between runs.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "data-hygiene", "name": "Data hygiene", "description": "How to treat nulls, duplicates, time zones and counts so the number means what the reader assumes it means.", "instructions": [{"text": "NULL is not zero. When a measure can be NULL, decide deliberately whether to exclude those rows or coalesce them, and say which you did -- AVG over a column with nulls answers a different question than the reader expects.", "priority": 60}, {"text": "COUNT(*) counts rows, COUNT(column) skips nulls, and COUNT(DISTINCT column) counts values. Pick the one that matches the question rather than the one that is shortest.", "priority": 55}, {"text": "A join that fans out multiplies the rows on the other side, so summing a parent's column after joining to its children double-counts it. Aggregate the child side first, or sum a column that belongs to the grain you are at.", "priority": 60}, {"text": "Timestamps are stored in UTC unless the schema says otherwise. Convert before grouping by day, because a day boundary in the wrong zone moves rows between buckets.", "priority": 50}, {"text": "When ordering by a measure to find the top or bottom, break ties explicitly. An ORDER BY with ties returns whichever row the engine happened to reach first, and the answer changes between runs.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.063766+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (7, 7, 1, $vanna$fb59247eedb720866e37e4f305c6e0720c65f59cf41de576e697cf384f9e5e39$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions for student records. The cohort rule is the one that most often
# turns a retention problem into a retention success, and it does it silently.

id: education-analytics
name: Education analytics
description: >-
  Census-date enrolment, cohorts that must not be redefined, and grades that
  are categories rather than numbers.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      Enrolment is counted as of a census date, not continuously. A count taken
      on any other day picks up late withdrawals and misses late registrations,
      and will not reconcile with anything the institution has published.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      A cohort is fixed when it is formed. Retention and completion follow the
      original members wherever they go; recomputing the cohort from whoever is
      still present is how a retention rate reaches one hundred per cent.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      A grade is often an ordered category rather than a number. Averaging
      letter grades needs a stated mapping, and a mapping that differs from the
      institution's own produces a figure nobody recognises.
    priority: 54

  - text: >-
      A student can be enrolled on several programmes and can attempt a course
      more than once. Say whether a figure counts students, enrolments or
      attempts, because all three answers look equally plausible.
    priority: 49

  - text: >-
      Small groups identify individuals even with no name in the result.
      Suppress any cell below an agreed threshold rather than publishing a
      group of two, and say that suppression was applied.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "education-analytics", "name": "Education analytics", "description": "Census-date enrolment, cohorts that must not be redefined, and grades that are categories rather than numbers.", "instructions": [{"text": "Enrolment is counted as of a census date, not continuously. A count taken on any other day picks up late withdrawals and misses late registrations, and will not reconcile with anything the institution has published.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "A cohort is fixed when it is formed. Retention and completion follow the original members wherever they go; recomputing the cohort from whoever is still present is how a retention rate reaches one hundred per cent.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "A grade is often an ordered category rather than a number. Averaging letter grades needs a stated mapping, and a mapping that differs from the institution's own produces a figure nobody recognises.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "A student can be enrolled on several programmes and can attempt a course more than once. Say whether a figure counts students, enrolments or attempts, because all three answers look equally plausible.", "priority": 49}, {"text": "Small groups identify individuals even with no name in the result. Suppress any cell below an agreed threshold rather than publishing a group of two, and say that suppression was applied.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.075403+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (8, 8, 1, $vanna$aea70895207cbd01a5e93bff691013c5c0826dc66669c9101c8c8dcadc4cb69e$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions that hold in most finance and billing schemas.
#
# A starter pack is a starting point, not a subscription. Enabling it copies these
# rules into the workspace, which then owns and may edit them; removing the pack
# later takes back only the copies nobody has touched.
#
# The first rule here used to be hardcoded into every new workspace by
# `Platform._seed_knowledge`. It is a business assumption -- true of a great many
# schemas and false in some -- so it belongs behind an explicit choice rather than
# being seeded into workspaces whose data it does not describe.

id: finance-conventions
name: Finance conventions
description: >-
  Currency units, fiscal calendar, and the difference between an invoiced amount
  and a recognised one.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      Monetary columns whose name ends in _cents store integer cents. Divide by
      100.0 and label the result in dollars.
    priority: 60

  - text: >-
      "Revenue" means the sum of line-level amounts unless someone asks
      specifically about invoice or order totals. The two differ whenever an
      invoice carries discounts, tax or shipping that are not attributed to a line.
    priority: 55

  - text: >-
      Exclude cancelled, refunded and test transactions from revenue figures
      unless the question is explicitly about them, and say in the answer that you
      did.
    priority: 55

  - text: >-
      A trailing twelve months window ends at the most recent complete month, not
      at today. A partial current month makes every trend look like a collapse.
    priority: 50
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "finance-conventions", "name": "Finance conventions", "description": "Currency units, fiscal calendar, and the difference between an invoiced amount and a recognised one.", "instructions": [{"text": "Monetary columns whose name ends in _cents store integer cents. Divide by 100.0 and label the result in dollars.", "priority": 60}, {"text": "\"Revenue\" means the sum of line-level amounts unless someone asks specifically about invoice or order totals. The two differ whenever an invoice carries discounts, tax or shipping that are not attributed to a line.", "priority": 55}, {"text": "Exclude cancelled, refunded and test transactions from revenue figures unless the question is explicitly about them, and say in the answer that you did.", "priority": 55}, {"text": "A trailing twelve months window ends at the most recent complete month, not at today. A partial current month makes every trend look like a collapse.", "priority": 50}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.084147+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (9, 9, 1, $vanna$229eff9ee53ae48365c62dcf2728284c860afb62b417f6a216caaffb1f9f4b73$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions for clinical and administrative health data. The privacy rules
# here are deliberately about how to report rather than about what is permitted
# -- that is a matter for the deployment, and a pack cannot know it.

id: healthcare-operations
name: Healthcare operations
description: >-
  Attendance against intent, the encounter as the unit of activity, and
  reporting clinical work without reporting a person.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      An appointment that did not happen still has a row, and a status column
      is what separates activity from intent. Count attendance explicitly
      rather than counting rows, and name the statuses that were included.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      Clinical activity can be reported without reporting a person. Aggregate
      to the clinician, department or period, and where a question cannot be
      answered without naming a patient beside a condition, say so rather than
      answering it.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      A small count identifies somebody even when no name appears in the
      result. Suppress cells below an agreed threshold and state that the
      suppression happened.
    priority: 54

  - text: >-
      The encounter is the unit of activity, and orders, results and charges
      hang off it. Counting any of them per patient needs the encounter in
      between, and one encounter can carry several of each.
    priority: 49

  - text: >-
      A clinician's speciality is not their seniority and is not their
      department. The three correlate closely enough to be mistaken for one
      another, so say which one a breakdown is by.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "healthcare-operations", "name": "Healthcare operations", "description": "Attendance against intent, the encounter as the unit of activity, and reporting clinical work without reporting a person.", "instructions": [{"text": "An appointment that did not happen still has a row, and a status column is what separates activity from intent. Count attendance explicitly rather than counting rows, and name the statuses that were included.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "Clinical activity can be reported without reporting a person. Aggregate to the clinician, department or period, and where a question cannot be answered without naming a patient beside a condition, say so rather than answering it.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "A small count identifies somebody even when no name appears in the result. Suppress cells below an agreed threshold and state that the suppression happened.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "The encounter is the unit of activity, and orders, results and charges hang off it. Counting any of them per patient needs the encounter in between, and one encounter can carry several of each.", "priority": 49}, {"text": "A clinician's speciality is not their seniority and is not their department. The three correlate closely enough to be mistaken for one another, so say which one a breakdown is by.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.094420+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (10, 10, 1, $vanna$6dc5f09817f2c5b07ca1683960688de253c02918b6e0b07ca31049e6c32d1a75$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions for accommodation data. Occupancy and revenue per available room
# are ratios whose denominators are easy to get wrong and impossible to spot
# afterwards, which is what most of this pack is about.

id: hospitality-operations
name: Hotels and hospitality
description: >-
  Occupancy denominators, rate against yield, and why a cancelled booking still
  carries a price.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      Occupancy is room nights sold over room nights available, so it needs the
      inventory as a denominator. A count of reservations divided by a count of
      rooms is not occupancy and will not agree with anything the property
      reports.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      Average daily rate is revenue over room nights sold; revenue per
      available room is revenue over room nights available. They answer
      different questions, and only the second one falls when the hotel is
      empty.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      A cancelled booking usually keeps its rate and its dates. Revenue has to
      exclude it while a cancellation rate has to include it, so the same table
      filtered two ways gives both figures.
    priority: 54

  - text: >-
      A stay is counted in nights, not days. Arriving and departing on the same
      date is zero nights, and the departure date is not a night sold.
    priority: 49

  - text: >-
      The date a booking was made and the dates it covers are different axes.
      Demand analysis groups by the booking date and occupancy by the stay
      date, and mixing them produces a seasonality curve that belongs to
      neither.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "hospitality-operations", "name": "Hotels and hospitality", "description": "Occupancy denominators, rate against yield, and why a cancelled booking still carries a price.", "instructions": [{"text": "Occupancy is room nights sold over room nights available, so it needs the inventory as a denominator. A count of reservations divided by a count of rooms is not occupancy and will not agree with anything the property reports.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "Average daily rate is revenue over room nights sold; revenue per available room is revenue over room nights available. They answer different questions, and only the second one falls when the hotel is empty.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "A cancelled booking usually keeps its rate and its dates. Revenue has to exclude it while a cancellation rate has to include it, so the same table filtered two ways gives both figures.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "A stay is counted in nights, not days. Arriving and departing on the same date is zero nights, and the departure date is not a night sold.", "priority": 49}, {"text": "The date a booking was made and the dates it covers are different axes. Demand analysis groups by the booking date and occupancy by the stay date, and mixing them produces a seasonality curve that belongs to neither.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.103112+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (11, 11, 1, $vanna$4596e04b8623bc2766b61a96114e06b5844743d9acca3666609ae1cdaec9530f$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions for shipment and delivery data. The first one is the rule most
# often broken by accident, and it flatters the operation every single time.

id: logistics-operations
name: Logistics and delivery
description: >-
  What on-time is measured against, transit versus dwell, and choosing between
  consignments, parcels and scans.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      On time means delivered against the promise made at booking, not against
      the latest estimate. Comparing with an estimate that was revised while
      the shipment ran late makes almost everything on time.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      Transit is movement and dwell is waiting. Total lead time is both, and a
      delay attributed to transit when it was really dwell sends people to fix
      the wrong part of the network.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      A shipment holds many parcels and a parcel is scanned many times. Decide
      whether the question is about consignments, packages or scan events
      before aggregating, because the three counts differ by an order of
      magnitude.
    priority: 54

  - text: >-
      A shipment still in flight has no delivery date. Dropping those rows
      measures only what has already arrived -- the population most likely to
      have arrived on time -- so count them as unresolved instead.
    priority: 50

  - text: >-
      Weight and volume are both billable and the larger of the two governs. A
      cost per kilogram that ignores volumetric weight understates the freight
      on anything light and bulky.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "logistics-operations", "name": "Logistics and delivery", "description": "What on-time is measured against, transit versus dwell, and choosing between consignments, parcels and scans.", "instructions": [{"text": "On time means delivered against the promise made at booking, not against the latest estimate. Comparing with an estimate that was revised while the shipment ran late makes almost everything on time.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "Transit is movement and dwell is waiting. Total lead time is both, and a delay attributed to transit when it was really dwell sends people to fix the wrong part of the network.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "A shipment holds many parcels and a parcel is scanned many times. Decide whether the question is about consignments, packages or scan events before aggregating, because the three counts differ by an order of magnitude.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "A shipment still in flight has no delivery date. Dropping those rows measures only what has already arrived -- the population most likely to have arrived on time -- so count them as unresolved instead.", "priority": 50}, {"text": "Weight and volume are both billable and the larger of the two governs. A cost per kilogram that ignores volumetric weight understates the freight on anything light and bulky.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.113087+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (12, 12, 1, $vanna$0f92bc696928f92a60f35eb1aeae09de55cea84fb654881d8e9c3e6cf4ea49e7$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions for production and shop-floor data. Each of these is a place where
# a reasonable-looking aggregate describes a healthier line than the one that is
# actually running.

id: manufacturing-operations
name: Manufacturing operations
description: >-
  Yield against output, downtime against planned running time, and where a
  rework unit gets counted twice.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      Units started and units accepted are different measures. Yield is
      accepted divided by started, so reporting output without scrap makes a
      failing line look productive.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      Downtime only means something against planned running time. A line idle
      because nothing was scheduled is not a failure, and averaging the two
      together hides the failures that matter.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      Rework is neither a good unit nor a scrapped one. Count it in its own
      right, or the same physical item is counted twice: once when it failed
      and once when it passed.
    priority: 54

  - text: >-
      A bill of materials is a hierarchy and component usage multiplies down
      its levels. Consumption per finished unit needs the whole explosion, not
      the top level's immediate children.
    priority: 49

  - text: >-
      A work order spans a date range and can cross a shift, a day and a
      reporting period. Attribute output to the period it completed in and say
      so, because attributing by start date moves output between months.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "manufacturing-operations", "name": "Manufacturing operations", "description": "Yield against output, downtime against planned running time, and where a rework unit gets counted twice.", "instructions": [{"text": "Units started and units accepted are different measures. Yield is accepted divided by started, so reporting output without scrap makes a failing line look productive.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "Downtime only means something against planned running time. A line idle because nothing was scheduled is not a failure, and averaging the two together hides the failures that matter.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "Rework is neither a good unit nor a scrapped one. Count it in its own right, or the same physical item is counted twice: once when it failed and once when it passed.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "A bill of materials is a hierarchy and component usage multiplies down its levels. Consumption per finished unit needs the whole explosion, not the top level's immediate children.", "priority": 49}, {"text": "A work order spans a date range and can cross a shift, a day and a reporting period. Attribute output to the period it completed in and say so, because attributing by start date moves output between months.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.125620+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (13, 13, 1, $vanna$ba1c1be98a605eacf0b5ce42b0c9f67b8b340091d59989d8699d2bdf3c2052ab$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions for store and basket data. Complements finance-conventions rather
# than repeating it: that pack defines what revenue is, this one defines the
# denominators retail measures get divided by.

id: retail-operations
name: Retail and storefront
description: >-
  Returns as negative sales, comparable-store rules, and the traffic
  denominator a conversion rate needs.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      A return is a negative sale, not a missing one. Net revenue subtracts
      returns in the period they came back, and reporting gross as though it
      were net overstates every category with a high return rate.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      Comparable growth compares only the stores trading in both periods.
      Including an opening or a closure attributes a change in the estate to a
      change in trade.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      The basket is the unit a shopper controls. Average basket value and items
      per basket are per transaction, so computing either per line inflates
      both.
    priority: 54

  - text: >-
      A conversion rate needs a traffic denominator from the same place and
      period as the transactions. Without one, report transactions and say that
      conversion cannot be computed from this data.
    priority: 49

  - text: >-
      Discounts, loyalty redemptions and gift cards each reduce what was
      collected without reducing what was sold. Say which of those two a
      revenue figure represents.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "retail-operations", "name": "Retail and storefront", "description": "Returns as negative sales, comparable-store rules, and the traffic denominator a conversion rate needs.", "instructions": [{"text": "A return is a negative sale, not a missing one. Net revenue subtracts returns in the period they came back, and reporting gross as though it were net overstates every category with a high return rate.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "Comparable growth compares only the stores trading in both periods. Including an opening or a closure attributes a change in the estate to a change in trade.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "The basket is the unit a shopper controls. Average basket value and items per basket are per transaction, so computing either per line inflates both.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "A conversion rate needs a traffic denominator from the same place and period as the transactions. Without one, report transactions and say that conversion cannot be computed from this data.", "priority": 49}, {"text": "Discounts, loyalty redemptions and gift cards each reduce what was collected without reducing what was sold. Say which of those two a revenue figure represents.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.135911+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (20, 20, 1, $vanna$9171d48e270662e7f37f709183223987956dad1c1916fe2ced47ae9c9153af20$vanna$, $vanna$# How the models join.
#
# Cardinality is not decoration: ONE_TO_MANY declared on [a, b] tells the
# compiler that an aggregate reached from a across this edge multiplies a's
# rows. Getting a direction backwards is how a semantic layer returns a
# confidently wrong number, so each is stated from the "one" side.
#
# The chain that matters here is invoice_lines -> tracks -> albums -> artists.
# There is no track.artist_id; joining a sale to an artist without going
# through albums returns nothing at all.

relationships:
  - name: artist_albums
    models: [artists, albums]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: artists.artist_id = albums.artist_id
    description: An artist has many albums.

  - name: album_tracks
    models: [albums, tracks]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: albums.album_id = tracks.album_id
    description: The only path from a track to its artist.

  - name: genre_tracks
    models: [genres, tracks]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: genres.genre_id = tracks.genre_id

  - name: media_type_tracks
    models: [media_types, tracks]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: media_types.media_type_id = tracks.media_type_id

  - name: track_sales
    models: [tracks, invoice_lines]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: tracks.track_id = invoice_lines.track_id
    description: Every sale of a track. Summing track.unit_price here would be wrong.

  - name: invoice_items
    models: [invoices, invoice_lines]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: invoices.invoice_id = invoice_lines.invoice_id
    description: >-
      An invoice has many lines, so invoices.total fans out across them. Use
      invoice_lines.line_revenue below invoice level and invoices.total at it.

  - name: customer_invoices
    models: [customers, invoices]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: customers.customer_id = invoices.customer_id

  - name: support_rep_customers
    models: [employees, customers]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: employees.employee_id = customers.support_rep_id
    description: >-
      The sales rep relationship. "Which rep sells the most" reaches revenue
      through this edge and then customers -> invoices.

  - name: playlist_entries
    models: [playlists, playlist_tracks]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: playlists.playlist_id = playlist_tracks.playlist_id

  - name: track_placements
    models: [tracks, playlist_tracks]
    join_type: ONE_TO_MANY
    condition: tracks.track_id = playlist_tracks.track_id
$vanna$, $vanna${"relationships": [{"name": "artist_albums", "models": ["artists", "albums"], "condition": "artists.artist_id = albums.artist_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY", "description": "An artist has many albums."}, {"name": "album_tracks", "models": ["albums", "tracks"], "condition": "albums.album_id = tracks.album_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY", "description": "The only path from a track to its artist."}, {"name": "genre_tracks", "models": ["genres", "tracks"], "condition": "genres.genre_id = tracks.genre_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY"}, {"name": "media_type_tracks", "models": ["media_types", "tracks"], "condition": "media_types.media_type_id = tracks.media_type_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY"}, {"name": "track_sales", "models": ["tracks", "invoice_lines"], "condition": "tracks.track_id = invoice_lines.track_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY", "description": "Every sale of a track. Summing track.unit_price here would be wrong."}, {"name": "invoice_items", "models": ["invoices", "invoice_lines"], "condition": "invoices.invoice_id = invoice_lines.invoice_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY", "description": "An invoice has many lines, so invoices.total fans out across them. Use invoice_lines.line_revenue below invoice level and invoices.total at it."}, {"name": "customer_invoices", "models": ["customers", "invoices"], "condition": "customers.customer_id = invoices.customer_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY"}, {"name": "support_rep_customers", "models": ["employees", "customers"], "condition": "employees.employee_id = customers.support_rep_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY", "description": "The sales rep relationship. \"Which rep sells the most\" reaches revenue through this edge and then customers -> invoices."}, {"name": "playlist_entries", "models": ["playlists", "playlist_tracks"], "condition": "playlists.playlist_id = playlist_tracks.playlist_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY"}, {"name": "track_placements", "models": ["tracks", "playlist_tracks"], "condition": "tracks.track_id = playlist_tracks.track_id", "join_type": "ONE_TO_MANY"}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.204618+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (14, 14, 1, $vanna$a1ded8a3067a921977b1627cbc2b57bcb1a541356a7601d8ab215947e31038fb$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions for stock and location data. Inventory is the classic case of a
# measure that is a level rather than a sum, and almost every wrong inventory
# number comes from treating it as the latter.

id: warehouse-inventory
name: Warehousing and inventory
description: >-
  On hand against available, reconciling a ledger with a snapshot, and why
  stock cannot be added up over a month.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      On hand is not available. Stock committed to an order is physically
      present and cannot be sold, so an availability figure that reports on
      hand promises inventory that is already gone.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      Inventory is a level at a moment. Stock for last month means the balance
      on a stated date, and adding up daily balances produces a number with no
      meaning at all.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      A movement ledger and a stock snapshot answer different questions and
      drift apart. Reconcile them before reporting either, and say which of the
      two a figure came from.
    priority: 54

  - text: >-
      The same item in two locations is two rows. Aggregate to the grain the
      question asks about, because a total that mixes site with bin counts the
      same pallet more than once.
    priority: 49

  - text: >-
      Days of cover compares stock against a consumption rate, so it needs a
      stated window for that rate. Computed against an unusual week it is a
      forecast of that week repeating.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "warehouse-inventory", "name": "Warehousing and inventory", "description": "On hand against available, reconciling a ledger with a snapshot, and why stock cannot be added up over a month.", "instructions": [{"text": "On hand is not available. Stock committed to an order is physically present and cannot be sold, so an availability figure that reports on hand promises inventory that is already gone.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "Inventory is a level at a moment. Stock for last month means the balance on a stated date, and adding up daily balances produces a number with no meaning at all.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "A movement ledger and a stock snapshot answer different questions and drift apart. Reconcile them before reporting either, and say which of the two a figure came from.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "The same item in two locations is two rows. Aggregate to the grain the question asks about, because a total that mixes site with bin counts the same pallet more than once.", "priority": 49}, {"text": "Days of cover compares stock against a consumption rate, so it needs a stated window for that rate. Computed against an unusual week it is a forecast of that week repeating.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.146508+00:00$vanna$);
INSERT INTO vanna_app.config_versions (id, config_file_id, version, checksum, raw_content, parsed, source, created_by, note, created_at) VALUES (15, 15, 1, $vanna$081476d2e72f1c710ad79cea921b5eead2be0e1e288de27cad7bc793c66cf125$vanna$, $vanna$# Conventions for HR and people data, written to be schema-agnostic so that they
# suit a workspace whose employee tables look nothing like the sample one.

id: workforce-analytics
name: Workforce analytics
description: >-
  Headcount as of a date, the denominator attrition needs, and dated attributes
  that each change on their own schedule.

instructions:
  - text: >-
      Headcount is a figure as of a date. A count over a period is either an
      average of daily headcount or the balance on a stated day -- never a
      count of everyone who appeared at any point, which counts a replacement
      twice.
    priority: 62

  - text: >-
      Attrition is leavers over average headcount for the same period, not over
      the closing figure. Dividing by the closing headcount makes a shrinking
      team's attrition look better the more people leave.
    priority: 58

  - text: >-
      Pay is personal. Report it as an aggregate over a group large enough to
      conceal an individual, and never beside a name unless the question is
      explicitly about one named person.
    priority: 54

  - text: >-
      A role, a grade and a reporting line each change on their own date. Their
      department means the one held on the date the question is about, which is
      rarely the one held now.
    priority: 49

  - text: >-
      Contractors, part-timers and people on leave are all employed, and none
      of them is one full-time equivalent. Say whether a headcount counts heads
      or FTE.
    priority: 45
$vanna$, $vanna${"id": "workforce-analytics", "name": "Workforce analytics", "description": "Headcount as of a date, the denominator attrition needs, and dated attributes that each change on their own schedule.", "instructions": [{"text": "Headcount is a figure as of a date. A count over a period is either an average of daily headcount or the balance on a stated day -- never a count of everyone who appeared at any point, which counts a replacement twice.", "priority": 62}, {"text": "Attrition is leavers over average headcount for the same period, not over the closing figure. Dividing by the closing headcount makes a shrinking team's attrition look better the more people leave.", "priority": 58}, {"text": "Pay is personal. Report it as an aggregate over a group large enough to conceal an individual, and never beside a name unless the question is explicitly about one named person.", "priority": 54}, {"text": "A role, a grade and a reporting line each change on their own date. Their department means the one held on the date the question is about, which is rarely the one held now.", "priority": 49}, {"text": "Contractors, part-timers and people on leave are all employed, and none of them is one full-time equivalent. Say whether a headcount counts heads or FTE.", "priority": 45}]}$vanna$, $vanna$import$vanna$, $vanna$seed_database$vanna$, NULL, $vanna$2026-08-25 06:23:10.155866+00:00$vanna$);
